WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.000
One. Four, five. Fourteen.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:39.000
As for the book. I’m going to tell it. They made this into a book, and when he sent it to be printed, Américo. But when he got older, he saw what he thought were mistakes. So, we had already been living for some time on Madison Street, West Madison. He brought us a big box, full.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:01:14.000
He told my wife that he wanted us to keep them there until he had time to come back because he was in [inaudible], because he wanted to destroy them. My wife took out about 10 or 15 and hid them. I kept one; the others I think one of my brothers took one and another one too.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.000
In some way a copy fell into the hands of Jesse Sloss and he told me he had it.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.000
You know, I think he passed away, I think.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.000
He already passed away? Was he sick?

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:39.000
Yes. I think so. I don’t know when, but I heard a gentleman who worked with him mentioned. Joe Rivera told me that, the county treasurer.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.000
He was sick in his feet.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:43.000
Yes, yes.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:56.000
I saw him at the mall, him and his wife. He was married to Tencha. I think her name was Tencha Hinojosa. But I didn’t know he had died.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.000
I think so. It seems so. I’ll find out, but I think so.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:32.000
He told me he had a copy. As I say, I have one here, but I don’t know whether to lend it, to allow you all to take a picture of the photo that’s there because according to Américo he didn’t like it anymore when he got older. He was embarrassed for people to read those verses of a boy in love.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:47.000
He wanted to destroy everything, but I kept one. I have it there. I don’t know whether to allow you to take a picture of that photo. Because it’s large.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:56.000
If there’s going to be a problem, just the photos you allow us, that’s fine. Because we don’t want you to get upset with him.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Yes, because if I allow you… I’m going to show it to you, but I don’t want you to take a photograph because I don’t want any controversy. I don’t want him to get mad and all that.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:17.000
Us neither. Better, if you don’t want to show it to me, I don’t even want to see it, that way there’s no problem. Just allow us what…

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:23.000
The ones I have there. The photographs I have there, I’ll show them to you in a moment. Mercedes!

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.000
I’ll bring them to you. They’re here on the table. Should I bring them?

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Ah well, bring it to me.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Yes. All right.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.000
They have a little paper, look, it’s there. He’s with his children and there’s the little paper, which is this one.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Very good. Thank you. We’ll start right now, Noro, whenever you’re ready.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:51.000
Up there it was… There should be a page, a loose one.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Yes. This one. The one that isn’t in color.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.000
What is that picture?

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.000
It’s a man and three children.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.000
Is it Américo?

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Yes. It’s Américo. It says, “January 1960”.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.000
Nineteen hundred what?

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.000
60.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:11.000
60.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:18.000
At Christmas. It says, “Christmas 59”. Yes, it must be Doctor Paredes.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.000
Is it him and two children?

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:27.000
Yes. The three children. The two children, one older and one is in his arms, little, and another.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.000
Are there others that Lydia looked for and are there?

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Yes. They’re marked here. Very good.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:47.000
Um, Mercedes? [Mercedes: Yes.] Bring me the book. The poems. [Mercedes: Okay]

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.000
You know you have these and there’s no problem, right, about making copies?

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.000
What?

00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:58.000
I mean…

00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:59.000
This? Yes.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Yes. That’s fine.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.000
These are portraits he took with the family.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.000
That’s fine.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:11.000
What I’m going to show you is what’s in the book. Is that the portrait?

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:12.000
Yes.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:14.000
It’s when he was young.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.000
You know. Look, look at this picture, it’s great. You can’t take it but…

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.000
Some poems, some of them don’t have the date.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.000
Yes. Let’s see. [Sound: Glass breaks off-screen]

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:31.000
What happened? Did something break?

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.000
Here it says, 3/1/36.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:42.000
’36?

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:50.000
Here it has the information of the year, the month and the day he wrote the poem.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:53.000
It says ’36?

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.000
Yes. ’36.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.000
He was already 21 years old.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.000
He was 21 years old?

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:17.000
Yes. Because he was born in ’15 and 10. 15 and 10, 25 and 5 is 30. It’s 15 and 6, 21. He was 21 years old. He was very young.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:34.000
Look, yes. You have a few copies, right? These are some of the few copies that exist?

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:51.000
It’s the only one I know of. I think there was one in the Texas Southmost College Library, but I don’t know who saw it there.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.000
Yolanda González should know if it’s there.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.000
What...Yolanda González?

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.000
Yolanda González. She should know if it’s there or not.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:03.000
She should know. It’s called… What does it say?

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:11.000
Cantos Adolescentes. You can get it there. I think it’s there.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.000
All right. I’ll ask the doctor to see if he allows us to use it.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:18.000
Yes.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:25.000
Mr. Américo and three children at Christmas. It’s in black and white.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.000
Well, do you want to take that one? Do you want that one? Look at the others that are there.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:42.000
Yes. Here’s one. Doctor Paredes and I don’t know who this man with glasses is and two children. Could it be his wife?

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:43.000
Yes.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:49.000
Is this his wife and two of the children?

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:51.000
Could be.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:09.000
Here’s one I think with you. There are five men. This one on the left, I don’t know if it’s your father or who it might be.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:13.000
There are five men?

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:18.000
Yes, there are five men. Three are on a sofa, and two are seated in front.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:23.000
Are they in a house, in a residence?

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.000
In a house. There’s a painting behind them.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:43.000
Ah, then they’re the… I had two brothers who were twins. Amador and Eleazar, and with Américo that made three. Eliseo made four and me, that’s five. It must just be us.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:44.000
Well there are five, there are five.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:45.000
There are five men?

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:48.000
Yes, the man in the middle has dark glasses.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:53.000
It must be Eliseo, my brother. The oldest.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:09:05.000
Are you on the right side? You’re wearing a tie. On the left side Mr. Paredes and his brothers here. What were the twins’ names?

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:19.000
Amador and Eleazar. They were twins. Amador was principal of Annie S. Putegnat.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:21.000
Yes, that’s true.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:29.000
Annie S. Putegnat. He died when he was principal there. He was principal there for 27 years.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:36.000
Don’t you have photos of Mr. Paredes when he was in the military, in the army?

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:37.000
Of whom?

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:40.000
Of Mr. Américo when he was in the army.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:59.000
You know, I do have some. I did have them, but my daughter didn’t find them. I had one where he was standing, in uniform with long pants. He’s alone, but I can’t find it.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:08.000
You can’t find it. That’s fine. Here’s one I think from when he graduated from Texas. In June 1956.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:09.000
Yes, doctor degree. It’s 1956, right?

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:11.000
1956, right.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:12.000
Well, you can take that one.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:18.000
He is in the center. You’ve got, I guess, the whole family there, or most of the family.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:24.000
Yes, there’s Mom, my wife, Amador’s wife and Amador is there too.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:25.000
Yes, there’s Amador.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:44.000
There’s Amador and there’s Ofelia. There’s Herman [inaudible], who was principal of Pace High School. His wife is there too. Me and my wife. Mom and Américo with the cap and gown.

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:46.000
Yes, he’s got the cap and gown.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:48.000
You can take this one too if you want.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:56.000
All right. In a moment you’ll give us their information and he’ll take the photos of them individually over here.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:01.000
Did you bring the camera?

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:07.000
Yes. We brought the camera. There’s a photo here that says 1944.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:08.000
It’s Américo.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:20.000
It’s a group of children. It looks like they’re at school. There are several here. I don’t know if one of you is here.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:25.000
If it’s not marked with the paper I don’t think it’s Américo.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:26.000
It’s not. That’s fine.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:58.000
Lydia was the one who… Since I can’t see, Mrs. Olivares who is here has only been with us three years, she doesn’t know the whole family. Américo, I don’t think she’s seen him. She’s never seen him. Because he’s sick all the time, almost most of the time. He rarely comes here.

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:10.000
Did she mark any of the three or four photos?

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:15.000
She marked them with little slips of paper she had. The papers are still there.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:20.000
Yes, are those the only ones you want us to take?

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:22.000
Yes, those are the ones she found.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:41.000
All right… Here’s a lady named Liliana Longoria Garza or Diana Longoria Garza.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:42.000
What’s her name?

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:43.000
Diana Longoria Garza.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:45.000
The first name?

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:50.000
I can’t see because there’s a piece… It’s Juana Longoria Garza.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:53.000
That sounds like Juanita, that’s my mother‑in‑law.

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:54.000
All right.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:03.000
She’s an old lady now, right? Yes.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:20.000
Is he sitting? Yes, he’s sitting. I think by the side of the road.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:23.000
It doesn’t have a date or anything?

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:28.000
Nothing. It doesn’t.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:30.000
I don’t know who it would be.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:51.000
Don’t you have other books that…? I have several that Mr. Américo wrote. Some, for example, graduation invitations or something like that that…

00:13:53.000 --> 00:14:00.000
Man, I think I do, but I’ll tell you it would take me a month.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:01.000
Yes, to look for them?

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:06.000
Yes, because there are so many things we have here.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:10.000
Yes, I understand. That’s fine. Is he about to turn 80?

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:18.000
Américo is 76. He was born on the 15th. Yes, he’s about to turn 80, right?

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:20.000
Yes, he’s going to turn 80.

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:39.000
In ’95 I’ll turn 80 in September. This September. In ’95 I’ll turn 80. Just on the 15th, 80, ’95. He’ll turn 80, he’s already 79.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:43.000
Did you attend school here in Brownsville?

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:44.000
Who? What about it?

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:46.000
You. Did you go to school here in Brownsville?

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:46.000
Yes.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:49.000
When did you graduate?

00:14:49.000 --> 00:15:10.000
I didn’t graduate from high school here. I took an exam many years ago and passed it. The professor who was there, he must have been the dean, his name was Philips. Is he still there?

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:12.000
No, he’s not there. Yes. I…

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.000
You saw him there? Philips.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:16.000
Yes, I worked with him.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:28.000
He wanted me to register for… No, I told him. I’m already… it was, you’ll see, I think it was around…

00:15:28.000 --> 00:16:01.000
’60. Around then. Because I went to Hammond. I had a lot of experience and I had read, I was working, I had worked in an Abstract? Company office and I had a lot of experience and practice in reading and interpreting legal documents and preparing legal documents.

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:50.000
By force—well, not by force, right? But I liked the work. I learned geometric formulas, how to get the length of a hypotenuse, get the volume of a cylinder, cubic inches and things like that I learned there on the job. I learned them just because I liked it. I have a book that, I’ll tell you the truth,

00:16:50.000 --> 00:17:17.000
I never studied it, I tried to read it when my eyesight was already bad. It was a school text of an uncle of mine named José María Manzano, who was born in Bagdad, but not Baghdad in Iraq. No, Bagdad here on the river. At the mouth of the river. Bagdad here at the mouth of the river.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:44.000
He was in school there and there he learned… There was a tower made of wood where they had the telegraph when he was a boy. That telegraph had two wires, they would send a message when a ship arrived and stopped way out there, because there was no port there. There was no entrance for ships.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:18:08.000
A ship would come and arrive way out there; with a spyglass they would see its name. They would telegraph Matamoros and there in the telegraph office they would notify the merchants in Matamoros that a ship had arrived, two ships, three ships, from such‑and‑such place. The name.

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:32.000
They would head out from here in carts and in wagons and on horses to pick up the merchandise. Because they brought merchandise from Europe. Here in Brownsville it wasn’t [inaudible]. My uncle José was born there, he lived there, went to school there and left that little book, I have it. It’s more than 100 years old. Imagine.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:40.000
I’ll bring it right now. Mercedes. Let’s see if she hears me. Mercedes?

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:45.000
All right. Mercedes, Mercedes?

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:47.000
Yes.

00:18:47.000 --> 00:19:01.000
Mercedes, you know where it is, there in the drawer. I think it’s in the middle drawer of the dresser.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:01.000
Yes.

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:14.000
There’s a big envelope. There are two envelopes, in one of them there’s another envelope where I have the little book called Elements of Geometry.

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:17.000
Do you want me to bring it?

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:22.000
Bring it to me. There are other things there too. The document for Pedro is there.

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:24.000
Yes.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:49.000
My grandfather grew up, was born here. His father’s name was Leandro Paredes. He had cows and horses, mares. There where… that’s what it was called at that time too. It’s still called La Parra Ranch. Have you heard of it?

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:50.000
Yes, I’ve heard of it.

00:19:51.000 --> 00:20:16.000
Which is right in front of where it says Sarita. Because Sarita, Sarita Kennedy, was Don Gregorio’s daughter, and Don Gregorio was Mifflin’s son, the owner of that whole ranch, La Parra. My father Leandro, before Mifflin Kennedy came, first rented and then bought from an heiress,

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:41.000
a lady named Loreto Villarreal de Capteville. He bought the right she had in two grazing lands, the La Parra range and the El Infiernillo range. Which is attached; La Parra borders the Los Olmos creek, which is where Sarita is. There’s Riviera. The Los Olmos creek.

00:20:42.000 --> 00:21:05.000
The other grazing land is called El Infiernillo, there where Riviera is. There my father Leandro bought what they called, they called it a right, and he bought La Parra too. There you go, this is it.

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:08.000
The book you mentioned?

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:08.000
Yes. Here it is.

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:19.000
Look.

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:42.000
Look at the dates, 1887, and it’s written on one of the pages by José. It says, “JM Manzano”. Manzano. Yes. Here it says Bagdad. Bagdad, 1887. ’87.

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:44.000
Yes. It says here.

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:58.000
It was the geometry textbook in Mexico and by the time they were in the fourth, fifth book they were already taking geometry.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:21:59.000
Yes. [Laughter]

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:01.000
And here they weren’t. [Laughter]

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:06.000
No, here not until ninth or tenth. [inaudible]

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:13.000
Did you notice the cover, the inscription? It says JM Manzano.

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:15.000
Yes, JM Manzano. Bagdad.

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:39.000
[On screen: A color photo of younger Americo Paredes seated with his wife and two sons around a birthday cake.]

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:39.000
Bagdad. I was at Colegio San José… Mercedes? [The video cuts to black]

